# Paddock — environment for the docker/ recipe.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` in this directory and fill in the values below:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored — NEVER commit real tokens. Compose auto-loads `.env`
# from this directory and injects these into the container.

# ---- Required: Claude authentication --------------------------------------
# Paddock shells out to the `claude` CLI. Authenticate with a Claude Max/Pro
# OAuth token: run `claude setup-token` on a machine where you're logged in and
# paste the result here.
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# ...OR use the Anthropic API (runtime: sdk) instead of Max/Pro — set this and
# leave the OAuth token above empty:
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ---- Optional: GitHub -----------------------------------------------------
# A GitHub token so keepers can clone private repos and push to backing repos.
# Scope it minimally (only the repos you want reachable).
# GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ---- Optional: Management API (remote MCP at /mcp) ------------------------
# Paddock v0.46.0+ serves a remote MCP endpoint at /mcp so a Claude Code session
# elsewhere can drive this instance. It is OFF by default: with no clients
# configured the endpoint 404s and does not exist.
#
# One variable per management client, holding that client's bearer token. It is
# REFERENCED from paddock.config.yaml (`ref: env:PADDOCK_MCP_TOKEN_LAPTOP`) and
# must never be written into that file — the config is git-tracked, so an inline
# value would leak into history. Paddock rejects an inline token outright.
#
# Mint one (>= 24 chars; the pdk_<instanceId>_ prefix binds it to THIS instance,
# so a copy is useless at any other Paddock):
#   printf 'pdk_%s_%s\n' myinstance "$(openssl rand -hex 24)"
#
# A client with no `scope:` is READ-ONLY. Granting writes grants code execution
# on this host — see the README's "Remote MCP" section, which also covers the
# TLS requirement (/mcp is refused over plaintext from non-loopback clients,
# and Docker's loopback publish does NOT count as loopback).
# PADDOCK_MCP_TOKEN_LAPTOP=

# ---- Auth / network binding -----------------------------------------------
# Paddock has NO built-in login. The compose file publishes to loopback only
# (127.0.0.1) and this defaults to `none`, so the instance is closed by default.
# PADDOCK_AUTH_MODE=none
#
# To expose Paddock beyond loopback, front it with a reverse proxy / auth
# sidecar and set a real auth mode (see the Securing Paddock guide):
# PADDOCK_AUTH_MODE=trusted-header
#
# NOTE: PADDOCK_DANGEROUSLY_ALLOW_OPEN is set to 1 inside docker-compose.yml,
# NOT here — a container must bind 0.0.0.0 internally, so the app's fail-closed
# guard needs it to boot. It is safe because publishing is loopback-only. See the
# README's Security section. You normally do not need to set it in .env.
